WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.620
- Do I believe that work
ethic is a foundational

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:04.690
piece of success?

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:05.880
Yes, I do.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:09.800
Do I want anybody ever to work so much

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.240
that they get depressed?

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:12.393
Of course not.

00:00:12.393 --> 00:00:14.976
(upbeat music)

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.350
- Gary V on Hustle Porn.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.540
- Look, I think that you
always have to sleep in the bed

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.760
that you make, and so
I'm aware that I've been

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.830
disproportionately passionate
over the last decade

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:47.830
around hustle and hard work,
like when I wrote "Crush It"

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.280
there was so much of that in
2009, I thought there was an

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.460
amazing opportunity of the
internet, YouTube, social media.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.810
That was true, people
benefit from it, both of you,

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:00.720
many who are watching.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.820
Yeah, I mean I think, look,
I think it's sad when you get

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:07.530
to a place in your career
where you can drive traffic

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:11.050
for others by just the
sheer mention of, and yeah,

00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.200
it's not fun to be the subject
of articles or keynotes

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.321
where people are taking
things and not painting--

00:01:17.321 --> 00:01:20.129
- [Interviewer] It's like bandwagoning.

00:01:20.129 --> 00:01:21.690
- It's just not the full picture.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.280
It's very clear, if you
read or watch anything

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.950
for even more than five
minutes that I want people

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:29.200
to be happy and be self-aware.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.470
Do I believe that work
ethic is a foundational

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:33.530
piece of success?

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:34.750
Yes, I do.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.630
Do I want anybody ever to work so much

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:40.000
that they get depressed?

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.250
Of course not.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:45.530
I never think that money is
the driver, and so I think

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:49.503
whether it's lazy headline
reading, or not doing homework,

00:01:49.503 --> 00:01:53.960
yeah it's not been the most
fun to get dragged into--

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.940
- No, yeah, I was like, (mumbles) no!

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:00.373
And there's so many people
who are proof of the words

00:02:00.373 --> 00:02:04.046
that you say, me included,
so yeah, it's like,

00:02:04.046 --> 00:02:05.385
oh, man, you just watch--

00:02:05.385 --> 00:02:09.770
- There's a byproduct
of success, you know?

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:13.422
Where people will, hustle porn's a term,

00:02:13.422 --> 00:02:15.884
leveragepreneur is a term.

00:02:15.884 --> 00:02:18.680
Leveragepreneur is when
you're using other people

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.320
to build yourself up at the
cost of the other person,

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:25.810
and you know, I think
that as you get bigger,

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.140
people will leverage you,
and I'm okay with that

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.910
because the truth always wins in the end.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:36.463
And I know the body of work
that I've put out, my intent,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:42.530
and if you wanna clip one thing
or one definitive statement

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.450
from one talk without the
context of the whole thing,

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.220
of course you can say,
anybody says anything.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:52.900
But the reality is that I
believe work ethic is important

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.450
if you wanna achieve hyperbolic success.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:58.510
I don't wanna tell people how to be happy.

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:00.963
So why are you telling
me how I should be happy?

00:03:00.963 --> 00:03:02.690
- [Interviewer] Mic drop.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:04.670
- And there's plenty of people
that are super depressed

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:06.640
that work 40 hours a week.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.510
It's just hyperbolized
link-bating, leveragepreneuring,

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:11.900
and I'm okay with that.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.340
But at the same token, it's
important not to not take

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.380
other peoples' points
of view into account,

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:20.694
and I'm in a constant
focus of that as well.

00:03:20.694 --> 00:03:23.277
(upbeat music)

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.180
A lotta people asked,
"How much do you sleep?"

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.360
And my answer's six, seven or eight hours,

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:30.620
and it fucks with people, right?

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.187
'Cause they think I'm
sleeping like an hour.

00:03:32.187 --> 00:03:33.720
(crowd laughs)

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.960
And my point there is, sleep's
important, like I don't know

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.990
who thinks they're gonna
survive without sleep.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.280
Sleep's important.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.000
What's important for what I'm
about is what you're doing

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.410
when you're awake.

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:49.047
I'm fascinated by people
who say, "I hustle,

00:03:49.047 --> 00:03:50.220
"I sleep four hours."

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.890
But then watch three hours of
YouTube videos a day, right?

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:57.150
And so for me, as you know,
and what you're referring to

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.530
as somebody who interacts with
me, is Tyler's sending you

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:04.770
emails like, "Can you do
11:30 p.m. to 12:30 p.m.?"

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:07.218
Every minute I have is programmed.

00:04:07.218 --> 00:04:11.350
- It's more like 11:15 to 12:25 (laughs).

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:13.490
- Yeah, no, like I mean,
the amount of five and seven

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:18.490
and 10 and 13, I basically,
Tyler, I see your face.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:20.673
What do you think?

00:04:20.673 --> 00:04:25.673
It's eight o'clock until 11
p.m. is pretty much programmed

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.360
to the minute with zero slots for anything

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.390
but the next meeting.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.210
- [Tyler] Yeah, 6 a.m. really.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:33.420
- But I'm working out, right?

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:38.420
But like, for work, eight
to 11 with zero lunch,

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.960
zero downtime, like if I go take a piss,

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:44.615
I'm cutting into some meeting.

00:04:44.615 --> 00:04:46.490
(crowd laughing)

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.940
Now, but here's the
thing, here's the thing.

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:52.320
That's matching my
happiness and the ambition.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:56.670
That is not for 99.9 percent of people.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.270
Time management needs
to be predicated on what

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.490
makes you happy and what is
coming out of your mouth.

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:04.910
The biggest thing I'm
worried about is people

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:09.710
are talking big game, but then
their actions don't match it.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:14.380
So to me, it's happiness first, and then

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:18.160
eliminating hypocrisy, and
that's how I think about it.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.310
So for me, it's that because
I love this game, it's what I

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:23.830
would wanna do, and my ambitions
are great, and I enjoy it.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.430
I want to spend 9 p.m. to 10
p.m. on a meeting to build

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:31.230
what I'm building, it's what I love to do.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.760
It's no different than being in the studio

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.835
or painting or, you know,
it's just what I love to do.

00:05:37.835 --> 00:05:40.418
(upbeat music)

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:45.210
- So first of all, it's
different for everybody.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.250
But what I would say hustle
is to me, is that when you

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:51.730
have passion around something,
that you are squeezing

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:56.040
every last bit of the juice
out of the orange, right?

00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.280
To me, hustle is maximizing the energy

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.410
you're putting into somebody.

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:03.910
I'm blown away by people
saying that they're hustling,

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:06.120
and they wanna achieve these great things,

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:07.940
and then they're actions don't match.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:10.230
It's like saying you
really wanna lose weight

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.290
while eating a Big Mac, right?

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:16.120
So to me, hustle would be
putting all your effort

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:18.020
into achieving the goal at hand.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.690
And for me that means
making every minute count.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:24.050
Hiring a full-time assistant who is...

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:27.890
Friday night didn't happen
because I was hustling like

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.440
every minute, like if I
miss my 15 minutes to tape

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.820
this show, I've missed
my 15 minutes because

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:34.470
there's so much hustle in my day.

00:06:37.121 --> 00:06:39.010
There's no hanging out.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:42.190
There's no time where like,
if I get to do like one joke

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:43.900
with you guys as I'm
walking in between meetings,

00:06:43.900 --> 00:06:47.140
we like find that to be a
rare acquaintance situation,

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.590
occurrence, not acquaintance.

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:51.390
Hustle is putting it all on the line.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.640
Hustle is waking up one
day, the day before you die,

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.190
and realizing you gave it
your all into the parenting

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.670
of your children, the
building of your businesses,

00:06:59.670 --> 00:07:03.110
the philanthropy that you wanted
to do, whatever you define,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:07.883
it's just all in, emotionally
and executionally.

00:07:07.883 --> 00:07:10.427
In theory and strategy and in execution.

00:07:10.427 --> 00:07:13.010
(upbeat music)

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.480
I get very upset when people
try to suppress people

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:22.320
from hard working without
realizing that that's what makes

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.329
that person happy and whether
it's me or Jason Fried

00:07:26.329 --> 00:07:29.950
and DHH, like I think we
actually agree on most things,

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:33.090
I think the thing we
probably would, I'm curious

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:37.870
in a sit down is, is it
up to us to know when Dan

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:42.130
or Johnny or Susan are
on the verge of burnout?

00:07:42.130 --> 00:07:46.080
And/or, do we know, telling
people you should work 40 hours

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:50.030
a week or 160 hours a
week, eliminates knowing

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.003
what they're about.

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:54.290
I don't know what else to say.

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:56.450
I think none of us get
to sit on an ivory tower

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:58.130
and tell people what to do.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.200
Do I believe that hard
work is an essential part

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:02.920
of the kinda success that people define?

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:03.760
I do.

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:06.790
Do I think working so many
hours that it burns you out,

00:08:06.790 --> 00:08:08.960
that you need to retire or quit

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:11.410
or you're unhappy in perpetuity is bad?

00:08:11.410 --> 00:08:12.380
Of course I do.

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.840
I think this is a self-awareness
game, and actually I think

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.040
it's a much more interesting
game of like, do you know why

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.810
you're working or what
are you trying to achieve?

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:24.010
I would go crazy sitting
on a farm in middle America

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:27.850
or doing 28 weeks of vacation,
it's not what makes me happy.

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.810
But I unbelievably am happy for the people

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:31.130
that makes it happy.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:33.110
I love the process of building something

00:08:33.110 --> 00:08:34.540
the way I wanna build it.

00:08:34.540 --> 00:08:38.150
I would go crazy having
all people working remotely

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.430
the way 37signals does.

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:43.970
They would go crazy managing
1000 people under a roof.

00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:45.831
I think it's about self-awareness.

00:08:45.831 --> 00:08:48.414
(upbeat music)

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:53.420
I've never actually never fallen asleep--

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:54.253
- You're a mutant.

00:08:54.253 --> 00:08:55.574
Okay, can we all agree--
- No I'm not sure.

00:08:55.574 --> 00:08:56.427
I'm gonna test myself.

00:08:56.427 --> 00:08:59.800
Honestly, by the way, I'm
getting a lot more seven and six,

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.770
seven and eight than
anybody would imagine.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:02.900
And on weekends?

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:04.340
I'll go 11.

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:06.800
I think sleep is game.
- Now we're getting the truth.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.060
Okay, so you actually give
yourself a lot of recovery.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:14.110
- What I'm doing is, Monday
through Friday, no question,

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:19.090
that game can be six and seven,
and when I play basketball,

00:09:19.090 --> 00:09:20.810
it can be even a little
earlier 'cause we have a 6 a.m.

00:09:20.810 --> 00:09:22.348
tip off, so I have to get up at 5:30,

00:09:22.348 --> 00:09:26.120
but I almost consistently
will try to make up time

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:28.300
Friday night to Saturday,
Saturday night to Sunday,

00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:30.870
Sunday, like I just don't know,

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.150
it's just I'm a big believer, that's all.

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:35.150
But now, I'm not worried
about what people,

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.660
how much sleep they get, I'm worried about

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:38.580
what they do while they're awake.

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.230
- Okay, but the two things
are connected because

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:45.710
if you wake up fully recharged,
it means you wake up ready

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:46.730
to take on the world.

00:09:46.730 --> 00:09:47.870
You know that feeling?
- Yes.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:51.110
- You wake up, and say
come on, bring it on,

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:54.840
it doesn't matter how many
obstacles, challenges, setbacks.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:56.240
- I prefer those things.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:57.360
- Okay, perfect.

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.100
Well, you know, Travis is a
friend of yours, I just joined

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.070
the board of Uber, Travis
has a little bit of that.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:07.080
It's like, he may be
dealing with 30 crises

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:08.280
at the same time--

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:09.300
- He eats it for lunch.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:10.870
- But what happens, and here's the key,

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:12.810
you just put something on
your Facebook yesterday

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:16.200
that I love which is, it's
ultimately just business.

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.100
- That's it.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.100
- It's not life and death.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:21.470
The people who have a
hard time are the people

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:24.660
who basically make it too important.

00:10:24.660 --> 00:10:25.837
Perspective is everything.

00:10:25.837 --> 00:10:28.420
(upbeat music)

